 Latent Sparse Attention: Towards Subquadratic
 Ultra-Long Context Attention 
 Ales Kubicek   *  1  
 Andrea Caciolai   *  1  
 Liangzu Peng   *  1  
 Sukrit Sumant   1  
 Benjamin Hoover   1  
 Fraser Greenlee   1  
 Ruairidh M. Battleday   1  
 Radim Urban   2  
 Torsten Hoefler   2  
 James C.R. Whittington   †   1  
 † † footnotetext: * Core contributors. † Senior author. 
 1 Prefrontal AI, London, United Kingdom
 2 ETH Zurich, Zurich, Switzerland.
Correspondence to: Ales Kubicek <ales@prefrontal.ai>, James C.R. Whittington <james@prefrontal.ai>.
 Preprint. 
 Abstract 
 Agentic workflows are increasingly shifting towards long-horizon tasks, such as open-ended scientific work, where models benefit from long context windows.
However, at ultra-long context (above 1M tokens), full attention is prohibitively expensive. A promising approach is sparse attention, in which a dedicated indexer selects a subset of keys for the attention. Yet current methods of indexing either still have quadratic cost in context size or lose accuracy at ultra-long context scale.
We address this with Latent Sparse Attention (LSA), an efficient attention method that maintains accuracy at ultra-long context. In LSA, keys are hierarchically clustered in a learned latent space, and queries score the hierarchy of clusters instead of scoring every key, making prefill, decode and training faster at long context.
We propose two variants, Distilled-LSA (D-LSA), which can be applied to pre-trained models via distillation, and Native-LSA (N-LSA), which is trained natively on the next-token loss alone.
On GLM-5.3-Flash ( Qwen-3.8-27B ) at 10M tokens, D-LSA speeds up one attention layer by 22.7 × 22.7\times ( 72.4 × 72.4\times ) in prefill and 4.6 × 4.6\times ( 42.5 × 42.5\times ) in decode over its original sparse attention (full attention), while keeping similar accuracy across a variety of tasks.
N-LSA matches the accuracy of full attention with a training speedup of 1.6 × 1.6\times per layer at 128k tokens.
LSA offers practical and efficient attention for models to benefit from ultra-long context. 
 1 Introduction 
 Figure 1: D-LSA and N-LSA overview. Top: one attention layer. The keys are pooled and clustered into buckets. A query ( ) scores the bucket centroids ( ) and attends only to the selected long-range keys (plus sink and window). Bottom: accuracy and performance (P: prefill, D: decode, T: training) for both D-LSA and N-LSA. D-LSA is shown with flat search. Its subquadratic variant, D-LSA (Tree), follows a similar trend (Figure  5 ). 
 Large language models have moved from answering single requests to agentic workflows in long-horizon tasks, such as software engineering and open-ended scientific research  ( Jimenez et al., 2024 ; Lu et al., 2026 ) .
These tasks keep growing in length  ( Kwa et al., 2025 ) , and models with longer context windows tend to score higher on them  ( Zhu et al., 2026 ) .
In line with this trend, recent models extend their context windows to 1M tokens and beyond  ( Gemini Team, 2024 ; MiniMax, 2025 ; DeepSeek-AI, 2026a ; Qwen Team, 2026a ) . 
 At this context size, attention dominates the cost, as its work grows with context  ( Vaswani et al., 2017 ; Dao et al., 2022 ) .
An established technique to reduce such costs is sparse attention  ( Hoefler et al., 2021 ) , in which each query attends to only a subset of the keys, chosen by a fixed pattern  ( Child et al., 2019 ; Beltagy et al., 2020 ; Zaheer et al., 2020 ) , by their content  ( Kitaev et al., 2020 ; Roy et al., 2021 ) , or by a dedicated indexer  ( Yuan et al., 2025 ; DeepSeek-AI, 2025 ) .
Recent frontier models are increasingly adopting sparse attention, in which keys are selected using a dedicated indexer  ( DeepSeek-AI, 2025 ; Qwen Team, 2026a ) . 
 However, current indexers still score every preceding key, or block of keys, for each query. This remains expensive (quadratic in prefill, linear in decode), and becomes a bottleneck at long context  ( Zan et al., 2026 ; Xu et al., 2026 ) . For example, at 10M tokens, 98% of the prefill time of a sparse attention layer is spent on indexing (Figure  2 ).
The cost of sparse attention at ultra-long context is therefore dominated by the cost of finding the keys. 
 To address this, we introduce Latent Sparse Attention (LSA), which clusters keys in a learned latent space and lets each query search the clusters instead of scoring every key. This can be done using only one level of clustering ( Flat ), or with a hierarchy of clusters ( Tree ), which makes the prefill subquadratic.
Furthermore, we propose two trainable designs: D-LSA, which adds LSA to pre-trained models and only trains small projection matrices per layer (contribution #1) , and N-LSA, which trains LSA natively with the Next-Token Prediction (NTP) loss alone (contribution #2) .
We evaluate state-of-the-art models ( Qwen-3.8-27B and GLM-5.3-Flash ) beyond their advertised context, up to 10M tokens (contribution #3) , on RULER  ( Hsieh et al., 2024 ) , which is an established long-context benchmark, and PaperTrail, which we developed to test long-context scientific understanding by finding and connecting facts across unseen scientific papers (contribution #4) .
Finally, we integrate LSA into SGLang  ( Zheng et al., 2024 ) 1 1 
 1 
   github.com/Prefrontal-AI/sglang with context parallelism, provide GPU kernels for H200 and B300, and release the code 2 2 
 2 
   github.com/Prefrontal-AI/latent-sparse-attention with all training and inference details (contribution #5) .
Figure  1 outlines our contributions. 
 2 Sparse Attention 
 In full attention, every query attends to every preceding key.
Prefilling a context of T T tokens therefore costs 𝒪 ⁡ ( T 2 ) \mathcal{O}(T^{2}) , and decoding one token costs 𝒪 ⁡ ( T ) \mathcal{O}(T) . In sparse attention, the work is split between an indexer and the attention. For each query, the indexer selects which keys the attention attends to 3 3 
 3 
 In this view, full attention corresponds to an indexer that returns the indices of all preceding keys. . With a fixed budget of k k keys, the attention then costs 𝒪 ⁡ ( T ​ k ) \mathcal{O}(Tk) to prefill and 𝒪 ⁡ ( k ) \mathcal{O}(k) per decoded token. If the indexer selects the most relevant keys, this is sufficient to stay close to the accuracy of full attention (Appendix  A ). 
 However, the indexer has a cost. Since it must score all preceding keys, it typically remains 𝒪 ⁡ ( T 2 ) \mathcal{O}(T^{2}) in prefill and 𝒪 ⁡ ( T ) \mathcal{O}(T) in decode, though with reduced constant factors. This makes ultra-long context still prohibitively expensive. For example, at 10M tokens the indexer takes 98% of the prefill time of traditional sparse attention (Figure  2 ), and a ‘free’ indexer would make sparse attention a further 58 × 58\times faster in prefill and 8.9 × 8.9\times in decode. 
 Figure 2: Traditional sparse attention at ultra-long context. One global attention layer of GLM-5.3-Flash (512k to 10M tokens) on one H200, prefilling the last chunk of 32,768 queries and decoding one token. Speedup of sparse attention over full attention ( blue ), share of the indexer ( % ), and potential speedup over sparse attention if the indexer were ‘free’ ( green ). 
 2.1 Design Choices 
 Sparse attention methods can be categorised by their indexer designs and how they are trained. 
 Token Sparse Attention. 
For each query, a lightweight indexer (fewer heads, smaller dimension, lower precision) scores every preceding key and selects which to attend to. 
 Block Sparse Attention. 
Consecutive keys are grouped into blocks, and each query scores blocks instead of every key.
All keys of the selected blocks are sent to the attention. 
 Semantic Sparse Attention. 
Keys are clustered by similarity rather than position, and each query scores one centroid per cluster.
