79: Agentic workflows are increasingly shifting towards long-horizon tasks, such as open-ended scientific work, where models benefit from long context windows.
80:However, at ultra-long context (above 1M tokens), full attention is prohibitively expensive. A promising approach is sparse attention, in which a dedicated indexer selects a subset of keys for the attention. Yet current methods of indexing either still have quadratic cost in context size or lose accuracy at ultra-long context scale.
81:We address this with Latent Sparse Attention (LSA), an efficient attention method that maintains accuracy at ultra-long context. In LSA, keys are hierarchically clustered in a learned latent space, and queries score the hierarchy of clusters instead of scoring every key, making prefill, decode and training faster at long context.
82:We propose two variants, Distilled-LSA (D-LSA), which can be applied to pre-trained models via distillation, and Native-LSA (N-LSA), which is trained natively on the next-token loss alone.
83:On GLM-5.3-Flash ( Qwen-3.8-27B ) at 10M tokens, D-LSA speeds up one attention layer by 22.7 × 22.7\times ( 72.4 × 72.4\times ) in prefill and 4.6 × 4.6\times ( 42.5 × 42.5\times ) in decode over its original sparse attention (full attention), while keeping similar accuracy across a variety of tasks.
84:N-LSA matches the accuracy of full attention with a training speedup of 1.6 × 1.6\times per layer at 128k tokens.
85:LSA offers practical and efficient attention for models to benefit from ultra-long context. 
89:These tasks keep growing in length  ( Kwa et al., 2025 ) , and models with longer context windows tend to score higher on them  ( Zhu et al., 2026 ) .
90:In line with this trend, recent models extend their context windows to 1M tokens and beyond  ( Gemini Team, 2024 ; MiniMax, 2025 ; DeepSeek-AI, 2026a ; Qwen Team, 2026a ) . 
91: At this context size, attention dominates the cost, as its work grows with context  ( Vaswani et al., 2017 ; Dao et al., 2022 ) .
94: However, current indexers still score every preceding key, or block of keys, for each query. This remains expensive (quadratic in prefill, linear in decode), and becomes a bottleneck at long context  ( Zan et al., 2026 ; Xu et al., 2026 ) . For example, at 10M tokens, 98% of the prefill time of a sparse attention layer is spent on indexing (Figure  2 ).
95:The cost of sparse attention at ultra-long context is therefore dominated by the cost of finding the keys. 
98:We evaluate state-of-the-art models ( Qwen-3.8-27B and GLM-5.3-Flash ) beyond their advertised context, up to 10M tokens (contribution #3) , on RULER  ( Hsieh et al., 2024 ) , which is an established long-context benchmark, and PaperTrail, which we developed to test long-context scientific understanding by finding and connecting facts across unseen scientific papers (contribution #4) .
