 Finally, we evaluate N-LSA, which is trained natively on the NTP loss alone. 
 Setup. 
We train a hybrid model of about 0.5B parameters with 24 layers, in which every fourth layer is an attention layer and the others are Gated DeltaNet layers  ( Yang et al., 2025b ) .
Each attention layer has 8 query heads over a single key-value head of 128 dimensions, without positional encoding.
We pre-train with a standard Warmup-Stable-Decay (WSD) learning rate schedule  ( Hu et al., 2024 ) , on 100B tokens of FineWeb-Edu  ( Penedo et al., 2024 ) at a context of 4k tokens in the Warmup and Stable phases, followed by 12B tokens of LongDocs in the Decay phase, at 128k tokens (Appendix  C ).
We compare N-LSA with Full Attention, Sliding Window Attention (SWA), and the native forms of block sparse attention, NSA  ( Yuan et al., 2025 ) for Block Sparse Attention (Flat) and PISA  ( Tang et al., 2026 ) for Block Sparse Attention (Tree), all in the same model architecture and on the same data.
Every sparse method attends to about 2,560 keys per query.
N-LSA selects k = 2048 k=2048 long-range keys with flat search, next to a window of 512 keys and 4 sink keys.
SWA attends to a window of 2,560 keys and 4 sink keys, NSA attends to 32 selected blocks of 64 keys and a window of 512 keys, and PISA attends to 40 blocks of 64 keys, which always include the first block and the two most recent blocks (window).
We train N-LSA with flat search only, and leave native training with tree search to future work. 
 Figure  7 shows that N-LSA reaches the validation loss of Full Attention throughout the decay phase at 128k token context, at 1.085 against 1.085, while PISA, NSA and SWA trail at 1.100, 1.117 and 1.154.
During pre-training at 4k tokens, all methods stay within 0.004 of each other, as their keys cover most of the context.
Table  4 confirms this on downstream tasks.
On commonsense reasoning  ( Clark et al., 2018 ; Zellers et al., 2019 ) , all models are on par.
On recall  ( Arora et al., 2024 ) , N-LSA stays close to Full Attention and ahead of the other sparse methods.
On RULER, N-LSA stays within 3.5 points of Full Attention up to 128k tokens, at 32.2 against 34.7 at 128k tokens, while NSA and PISA fall to 19.2 and 14.6, and SWA to 3.0 once the context outgrows its window. 
 N-LSA also trains faster than Full Attention at long context.
For one attention layer on a single GPU, the forward and backward pass of N-LSA is 1.1 × \times faster than Full Attention with FlashAttention-3  ( Shah et al., 2024 ) at 64k tokens and 1.6 × \times faster at 128k tokens (Figure  7 , left).
End to end, the whole model completes its decay phase 1.24 × \times faster at the same validation loss (Figure  7 , right), as only every fourth layer is an attention layer.
At shorter contexts, the selection of the keys costs more than it saves.
Its peak memory is higher, at 6.8 GiB against 5.8 GiB per layer at 128k tokens, partly because N-LSA keeps the indices of the selected keys of every query for the backward pass, which grow linearly with the context. 
 Overall, N-LSA trains natively at the accuracy of Full Attention and becomes faster as the context grows. 
 5 Discussion 
 LSA reduces the cost of finding the keys, but not of storing them, and the KV cache still grows linearly with the context.
It is orthogonal to methods that compress the KV cache  ( DeepSeek-AI, 2026a ) , share the index across layers  ( Bai et al., 2026 ; DeepSeek-AI, 2026b ) , or predict several tokens per step  ( Gloeckle et al., 2024 ; DeepSeek-AI, 2024 ) , and could be combined with each. 
 We observed tree search to lose accuracy faster than flat search past 1M tokens on GLM-5.3-Flash , while it overtakes flat search in decode only from about 5M tokens with one request, and is predicted to do so in prefill only beyond 10M tokens.
It remains open whether sparse training would close this gap beyond indexer distillation. 
 We train N-LSA at a small scale, with about 0.5B parameters, up to 128k tokens and with flat search only.
Scaling native training to larger models, longer contexts and tree search remains open. 
 6 Conclusion 
 At ultra-long context, the cost of sparse attention lies in finding which keys to attend to.
We introduce LSA, which clusters the keys in a learned latent space and replaces the scan of every key with a search over the clusters, flat or along a tree.
D-LSA distils LSA into pre-trained models and stays close to the accuracy of their baseline attention up to 10M tokens.
At 10M tokens, one attention layer of D-LSA prefills 22.7 × \times and decodes 4.6 × \times faster than sparse attention in GLM-5.3-Flash , and 72.4 × \times and 42.5 × \times faster than Full Attention in Qwen-3.8-27B .
End to end, this reduces the time to first token by 2.2 × \times and 9.2 × \times , respectively.
N-LSA trains LSA natively on the next-token loss alone, at the accuracy of Full Attention and 1.6 × \times faster per layer at 128k tokens.
With tree search, the cost of finding the keys grows subquadratically, which makes LSA a step towards attention that scales well beyond 10M tokens.
LSA thus provides a practical way forward for ultra-long context attention, both for distilling pre-trained models and for native training. 
 Acknowledgements 
 We thank Daniel Woods, Roman Iurkov, Cyril Kondratenko, Manuel Gomez, and the rest of the Nebius team for excellent support with the compute platform. 
 References 
 Alabi et al. (2012) 
Alabi, T., Blanchard, J. D., Gordon, B., and Steinbach, R.
 Fast k k -selection algorithms for graphics processing units.
