For each query, a lightweight indexer (fewer heads, smaller dimension, lower precision) scores every preceding key and selects which to attend to. 
 Block Sparse Attention. 
Consecutive keys are grouped into blocks, and each query scores blocks instead of every key.
All keys of the selected blocks are sent to the attention. 
 Semantic Sparse Attention. 
Keys are clustered by similarity rather than position, and each query scores one centroid per cluster.
All keys of the selected clusters are sent to the attention. 
 For the last two categories, a query can search the groups in three ways. Flat search scores every group directly, which is 𝒪 ⁡ ( T ) \mathcal{O}(T) work per query. Fixed search adds a shallow hierarchy and refines from coarse to fine groups, which is also 𝒪 ⁡ ( T ) \mathcal{O}(T) per query. Tree search descends a deeper hierarchy and expands only the groups it keeps at each level, which is 𝒪 ⁡ ( log ⁡ T ) \mathcal{O}(\log T) per query. 
 An indexer can be training-free, distilled from a pre-trained model, or trained natively on the NTP loss alone. 
