We stop the gradient through the selection, and the NTP loss trains the attention projections only through the keys each query attends to, the selected keys plus the sink and the window.
N-LSA therefore adds no parameters to the model and needs no teacher, no index projections and no auxiliary loss. 
 The attention layers of N-LSA use no positional encoding, as is becoming more common in hybrid models, and RoPE could be supported by clustering only the RoPE-free part of the keys, as D-LSA does on Qwen-3.8-27B . 
 4 Evaluation 
 In this section, we describe the models, the methods we compare, and their settings.
Then, we evaluate the accuracy of D-LSA up to 10M tokens, and its performance per attention layer and end to end.
Finally, we show that N-LSA can be trained natively at an accuracy close to full attention. 
 4.1 Setup 
 Models. 
D-LSA aims to keep the accuracy of a model while making its attention significantly cheaper. We compare each model with D-LSA against its baseline attention, in accuracy and in performance.
We demonstrate D-LSA on both a model with full attention ( Qwen-3.8-27B   ( Qwen Team, 2026b ; Qwen Team, 2026c ) ) and one with sparse attention ( GLM-5.3-Flash   ( Z.ai, 2026 ) ). On Qwen-3.8-27B , we also replace the attention with block sparse attention, inspired by QSA  ( Qwen Team, 2026a ) , as a second sparse baseline. We also compare flat to tree search where possible. 
 Methods. 
We compare the following methods: 
 • 
 Full Attention : every query attends to every preceding key, the baseline attention of Qwen-3.8-27B . 
 • 
 Block Sparse Attention (Flat) : sparse attention whose indexer scores every block of four consecutive keys, as in QSA  ( Qwen Team, 2026a ) and GLM-5.3-Flash   ( Z.ai, 2026 ) . 
 • 
 Block Sparse Attention (Tree) : sparse attention whose indexer searches a tree over blocks of the keys of the attention itself, as in HiP  ( Lee et al., 2025a ) and PISA  ( Tang et al., 2026 ) . To represent these methods, we search a tree over blocks of keys directly on the KV cache, without training, which uses the full keys and favours accuracy over performance. We use blocks of eight keys, the most accurate of the sizes we compare (Appendix  E.6 ). 
 • 
 D-LSA (Flat) and   D-LSA (Tree) : D-LSA with a flat search over every bucket centroid and with a tree search over the hierarchy of buckets.
In decode, each request processes one token per step, and the tree search runs its levels one after another, which pays off only when many requests decode together.
As we evaluate one request at a time, D-LSA (Tree) decodes with the flat search (Appendix  F.1 ). 
 • 
 D-LSA (Training-Free) : D-LSA (Flat) with the principal directions of the keys as both projections instead of trained ones. 
 Every trained indexer we compare is distilled in the same way to ensure fairness. 
 Design parameters. 
Table  2 lists the parameters of every method per model.
For D-LSA, we found that a budget of k = 4096 k=4096 long-range keys holds accuracy past 1M tokens better than smaller budgets, and these extra keys cost little next to the scan of the indexer they replace.
On GLM-5.3-Flash , D-LSA (Flat) selects k = 2048 k=2048 long-range keys for queries before position 1M and k = 4096 k=4096 beyond.
We found that the smaller budget keeps accuracy below 1M tokens while making the attention faster.
Tuning the budget further, for example per layer or per request, is left to future work.
A small index space of d ( idx ) = 64 d^{(\mathrm{idx})}=64 dimensions keeps the scoring and the clustering cheap, and we found wider spaces to add little accuracy. 
