 E.3 Reasoning Length 
 Figure 12: Reasoning tokens against baselines. 
Reasoning tokens of each method relative to the baseline of its model, on the same questions.
Grey bands mark the expected variation, and markers inside them show no measurable change in reasoning length. 
 Figure  12 compares the reasoning length of each method with the baseline of its model, question by question.
For each question, we average the reasoning tokens over its repeats and divide them by those of the baseline on the same question, and each marker is the geometric mean of these ratios over the benchmark.
The grey band of a benchmark is the median width of the 95% bootstrap intervals of its methods, and a marker inside it shows no measurable change.
Every D-LSA design stays inside its band, except D-LSA (Tree) on GPQA-Diamond with Qwen-3.8-27B , which reasons 11% shorter. 
 E.4 RULER 
 Figure  11 shows the 13 tasks of RULER from 4k to 10M tokens, grouped into retrieval, tracing, aggregation and question answering.
Single-needle retrieval stays at 95 or above for every method up to 5M tokens, except Block Sparse Attention (Tree), which falls to 88 and 71 at 1M tokens.
MultiKey 2, MultiKey 3 and CWE fall to at most 5 at 10M tokens for every method, including the baselines, and bound the RULER average of every method.
On Qwen-3.8-27B , D-LSA (Flat) exceeds baseline Full Attention at 10M tokens mostly on MultiQuery and HotpotQA, at 92 and 35 against 72 and 20.
On GLM-5.3-Flash , D-LSA (Tree) falls to 60, 56 and 55 on MultiKey 1, MultiValue and VT at 10M tokens, against 100 for baseline sparse attention, and these three tasks account for most of its gap to D-LSA (Flat). 
 E.5 PaperTrail 
 Figure 13: D-LSA accuracy on PaperTrail per tier. 
Tier 1, tier 2 and tier 3 questions, each averaged over the three domains and three repeats. 
 Figure  13 shows the three tiers of PaperTrail and their average from 512k to 10M tokens.
The harder the tier, the earlier it falls.
Tier 3 questions fall to at most 19.0 at 10M tokens for every method on both models, while tier 1 questions stay above 90 on GLM-5.3-Flash .
On Qwen-3.8-27B , D-LSA exceeds baseline Full Attention from 5M tokens on tier 1 and tier 2 questions, and D-LSA (Flat) reaches 53.3 and 32.2 against 32.2 and 22.2 at 10M tokens.
On GLM-5.3-Flash , D-LSA trails baseline sparse attention mostly on tier 3 questions from 2M tokens, and D-LSA (Flat) also on tier 2 questions at 10M tokens, at 56.7 against 76.7. 
 Table  8 further reports the questions whose answer is not in the papers, at 512k tokens.
Every D-LSA design recognises these questions within 3.7 points of the baseline of its model, at 96.3 to 100.0 against 100.0 for Qwen-3.8-27B and 96.3 for GLM-5.3-Flash . 
